# Example 18: Genetic toggle switch - synthetic bistable memory (Gardner, Cantor & Collins 2000)
# Two repressors arranged in a mutually inhibitory network form a synthetic,
# bistable switch: an addressable cellular memory unit.
#
# The pIKE-class toggle uses:
#   Ptrc-2  (LacI-repressible)  driving tetR
#   PLtetO1 (TetR-repressible)  driving lacI
# with a GFPmut3 reporter. The switch is flipped between stable states by a
# transient pulse of IPTG (2 mM, inactivates LacI) or anhydrotetracycline
# (aTc, 500 ng/ml, inactivates TetR), and then stays in that state for the
# rest of the culture.
#
# Measured parameters (Gardner, Cantor & Collins, Nature 403:339-342, 2000,
# DOI:10.1038/35002131):
#   - bistable for 5 of 6 toggle plasmids tested; the monostable variant
#     (pIKE105) had an unbalanced TetR/PLtetO1 ratio -> promoter balance
#     determines bistability
#   - nearly ideal switching threshold: the induction curve crosses the
#     bifurcation at ~40 uM IPTG (pTAK117)
#   - ranked promoter transcription efficiencies: PLs1con > Ptrc-2 > PLtetO1
#   - hysteresis: a 6 h IPTG pulse holds the state for >= 22 h after removal
#
# HelixLang declares the mutually inhibitory topology below. Genuine bistability
# requires the cooperative Hill repression and leaky basal synthesis of Gardner's
# model equation (1); the discrete GRN without those terms relaxes to a single
# fixed point from either initial state, so the Python API shows the topology.

#promoter name=p_tet_repressed strength=0.7   # Ptrc-2: strong, LacI-repressible
#promoter name=p_lac_repressed strength=0.9   # PLtetO1: strong, TetR-repressible

#gene name=lacI promoter=p_tet_repressed
ATG GCT GCT GCT TAA
#end

#gene name=tetR promoter=p_lac_repressed
ATG GCT GCT GCT TAA
#end

#regulate lacI -> p_lac_repressed strength=-0.9
#regulate tetR -> p_tet_repressed strength=-0.9

